On Apr 26, 2007, at 1:54 PM, Qingsong Yang wrote:

> asInt() returns 0 if the REXP is no integer, what if the value being
> evaluated is exactly 0?
> Same concern over asDouble() returns 0.0 if the REXP is no double,  
> what if
> the value being evaluated is exactly 0.0?
> Why can't these methods return null?

Ask Java designers ... double/int are scalar types so they cannot  
return null. Recently JRclient was in fact updated to return NaN at  
least for the double case. As of int, we could introduce a new  
extreme value that represents NULL, analogous to NA in R.

BTW: regardless of what the returned value is, the assumption is that  
you know that the result is int/double and it should be checked with  
isNumeric or isInteger if it may be something else.

Cheers,
Simon

